{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And Moses heard the people weeping for their families, every man at the door of his tent.</b> For one cannot say that they gathered family by family and wept — since it is stated, 'every man at the door of his tent' — but rather it was certainly on account of family matters. 'And the anger of HaShem was kindled, and it was evil in the eyes of Moses' — specifically now; but beforehand it was evil in the ears of HaShem alone, for Moses did not grasp their intention. And what they said, 'we remember,' connotes the past, while 'which we ate' connotes the future. So they said: at every time when we said for the past, 'Let us set a leader and return to Egypt,' at that time 'we remember the fish which we ate' — for the future, when we arrive there. And what they said — 'for nothing' — meaning free from the commandments, will be explained with the help of HaShem below in the portion of Pinchas (26:64), that it was concerning terumot and ma'aserot that they said thus." "<b>And it ate at the edge of the camp.</b> Literally at the edge. Therefore all the people fled from the edge when they saw that all around the camp it burned fiercely, and they fled into the interior of the camp, for they were afraid to be at the edges. Therefore it is stated, 'and the asafsuf that was in its midst' — we find nowhere in all the Torah that the erev rav were called asafsuf except here, because 'asafsuf' is a doubling: sof-sof, meaning all the people found at every end and extremity of the camp, who, seeing that fire burned around them, fled into the interior of the camp. This is what it says: 'that was in its midst' — in the middle, as said." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sifrei 11a) interpreted: among the ketzinim of the camp, or among the muktzim of the camp. And the expression is not clear to me, for every ketz is not the language of katzin, nor the language of muktzeh. And it appears to me that all agree that ketz does not depart from its plain sense, that it speaks of the ketz of the camp, for every ketz is the language of sof. But every edge has two ends, a head and a tail. And according to the way of the world, the ketzinei am walk at the head of the camp — that is the first ketz — and the muktzim walk at the rear of the camp — that is the last ketz. And according to all opinions, ketz of the camp does not depart from its plain sense; only that Scripture did not specify which ketz — whether among the ketzinei am who walk first at the first ketz, or among the muktzim of the camp who walk at the last ketz. Therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory disagreed on the matter: some say among the ketzinim, and some say among the muktzim. And their dispute depends on what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Bava Kamma 60a): punishment does not come upon the world except on account of the wicked, and it does not begin except with the righteous, as it is stated (Exodus 22:5), 'When a fire goes forth and finds thorns, and a stack of grain is consumed' — the stack has already been consumed. According to this, the one who says 'among the ketzinim of the camp' holds that this verse speaks of those with whom the punishment begins, and the one who says 'among the muktzim' holds that this verse speaks of those who cause the punishment. But from the fact that it says 'and the asafsuf' — a doubling of sof — infer from it that it speaks of both: of the ketzinim and of the muktzim, for both are at the sof: this at the first sof and this at the last sof. And so the language of Scripture implies: 'and found thorns (קֹצִים),' that is, two ketzot, for kotzim is the language of ketz; 'and a stack of grain is consumed' — everything is included, even the average folk in the middle. Therefore it says 'and the asafsuf,' that is, two sofs; 'that was in its midst (אֲשֶׁר בְּקִרְבּוֹ),' as though it said 'and (וַ) that which was in its midst' — that is, the average folk — just as (Habakkuk 3:11) 'sun (שֶׁמֶשׁ) moon (יָרֵחַ) stood still in their abode,' missing a vav, as if it said 'sun and moon' — so too here it is as if it said 'and the asafsuf and that which was in its midst': all of them together were stricken, from the sole of the foot to the head; all of them craved a craving." "And according to the way of midrash, one may say that such is the measure of the Holy One, blessed be He: when the creatures are wicked and sinful, then the Holy One, blessed be He, lays hold of both ends — among the good and among the wicked — so that all the average folk will take reproof by a fortiori reasoning. For if He touched only the lowly, then the good among them would rely on their good deeds and say: the evil will not reach us, for we are not sinners like them. Therefore He touches also the blameless among them, and then the lowly will take from their own measure a fortiori and say: if fire seized the moist, what shall the dry thorns do? And if He touched only the good among them, the lowly would say: well do these deserve punishment, for they are close to HaShem, and around Him it burns fiercely — for whoever draws nearer to HaShem must be more precise in his deeds, for one who rebels against the king inside his palace is not like one who rebels outside his palace. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, seizes both ends, and then all these arguments are nullified, and all that is between the ends will fear HaShem and not return to presumption. And this matter is stated explicitly by Ezekiel (Ezekiel 15:2–4): 'Son of man, what will the wood of the vine be… its two ends the fire consumed, and its interior is charred — is it fit for any work?' For he compared Israel to a vine whose two ends the fire consumes and whose interior is charred and shaken. So too the Holy One, blessed be He, seizes the asafsuf — the ketzinim and the muktzim — so that those who are in the midst become charred and afraid. And when all of them turned aside from the way, then the fire consumed all of them — whether the asafsuf or those in its midst, that is, its interior. And perhaps the ketzinim are not the good ones, but rather the hypocrites who show themselves as fitting so that they will be appointed as heads and officers, while in their midst they plant their wickedness. And the Holy One, blessed be He, exposed the hypocrites and caused their evil to come out into the open, for 'he who conceals hatred with his mouth — his evil will be revealed in the assembly' (Proverbs 26:26)."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {1 "The wordplay on אספסף as a reduplication of סוף (sof-sof, 'end-end') is central to the comment and preserved in translation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:59.957144523Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}